.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt UNIX 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm unix
.Nd sockets for communication between processes of one machine
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/socket.h
.In sys/un.h
.Ft int
.Fn socket "AF_UNIX" "int type" "0"
.Ft int
.Fn socketpair "AF_UNIX" "int type" "0" "int sv[2]"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
An
.Dv AF_UNIX
socket carries data between processes on this machine.
.Fa type
is
.Dv SOCK_STREAM ,
a byte stream between two connected ends;
.Dv SOCK_SEQPACKET ,
the same connection with the boundaries of messages kept; or
.Dv SOCK_DGRAM ,
messages sent to a name, or to the socket connected to.
.Dv SOCK_RAW
is taken as
.Dv SOCK_DGRAM ,
as in Linux, and
.Dv SOCK_CLOEXEC
and
.Dv SOCK_NONBLOCK
may be added to the type.
.Fn socketpair
makes two sockets connected to each other.
.Ss Names
A name is a
.Vt struct sockaddr_un :
.Dv AF_UNIX
and
.Fa sun_path ,
108 bytes.
A path in
.Fa sun_path ,
which ends at its first NUL byte or at the end of the address, names a
socket file:
.Xr bind 2
makes it, with the permission bits 0777 less the umask, and fails with
.Er EADDRINUSE
when the path exists; a connect or a send to it needs write permission on
it.
The file stays after the socket is closed, as in Linux, and a connect to
it then fails with
.Er ECONNREFUSED .
A name whose first byte is NUL is in the abstract namespace: it names no
file, lasts as long as its socket, and is found only by sockets of the
same type.
A bind with an address of the family alone, or a connect or send by a
socket with
.Dv SO_PASSCRED
and no name, gives the socket an abstract name of five hexadecimal
digits.
.Xr getsockname 2
reports a path with its NUL in the length; an accepted socket reports
its listener's name.
.Ss Connections
.Xr listen 2
makes a bound stream or sequenced-packet socket accept connections; its
backlog is at most 4096.
.Xr connect 2
queues a connection at once, and the connecting socket may send before
it is accepted; with the queue past the backlog, a connect waits, or
fails with
.Er EAGAIN
when the socket is nonblocking.
.Xr accept4 2
takes the oldest queued connection.
When one end of a connection closes, the other reads the end of input and
a send there fails with
.Er EPIPE
and
.Dv SIGPIPE ;
if the closing end left data unread, or was never accepted, the other end
also gets
.Er ECONNRESET
once.
.Xr shutdown 2
ends the input or output of one end, and the matching direction of the
other.
.Ss Data
A stream is sent in pieces of at most 64 KiB as the receiver's queue has
room, at most 212992 bytes; a read returns what is queued, across the
pieces, and waits only for the first byte, or with
.Dv MSG_WAITALL
for all.
A sequenced-packet or datagram receive takes one message; bytes past the
buffer are dropped and
.Dv MSG_TRUNC
set, and with
.Dv MSG_TRUNC
in the flags the call returns the message's whole length.
A datagram receiver queues at most 10 messages and 212992 bytes, and a
sender waits for room, or fails with
.Er EAGAIN .
A datagram socket may be connected to one peer; a socket connected to
another refuses the send with
.Er EPERM .
.Dv MSG_PEEK ,
.Dv MSG_DONTWAIT
and
.Dv MSG_NOSIGNAL
behave as in Linux;
.Dv MSG_OOB
fails with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
.Ss Control messages
A
.Xr sendmsg 2
may carry
.Dv SCM_RIGHTS ,
up to 253 descriptors, which the receiver gets as new descriptors of the
same open files, with
.Dv FD_CLOEXEC
when it passed
.Dv MSG_CMSG_CLOEXEC .
On a stream, the descriptors arrive with the first byte of the send that
carried them, and a read that takes them ends after that send's bytes.
Descriptors for which the receiver's control buffer has no room are
closed, and
.Dv MSG_CTRUNC
is set.
A socket that is itself sent over itself and never received is not
reclaimed; Linux collects such cycles.
.Pp
A receiver with
.Dv SO_PASSCRED
gets
.Dv SCM_CREDENTIALS
with every message: the sender's process id and real user and group ids,
or those the sender gave, which must be its own real, effective or saved
ids unless it is root.
On a stream with
.Dv SO_PASSCRED ,
a read does not join the bytes of two senders.
.Ss Options
.Dv SO_PEERCRED
reports the process id and effective ids of the peer when it connected,
or of the listener when it began to listen; with no peer, pid 0 and ids
-1.
.Dv SO_TYPE ,
.Dv SO_DOMAIN ,
.Dv SO_PROTOCOL ,
.Dv SO_ERROR ,
.Dv SO_ACCEPTCONN ,
.Dv SO_SNDBUF
and
.Dv SO_RCVBUF
are reported; a buffer size set is doubled, as in Linux, from 4608 to
425984 bytes.
.Dv SO_RCVTIMEO
and
.Dv SO_SNDTIMEO
bound a wait, rounded up to 10 ms ticks, which then fails with
.Er EAGAIN .
.Dv SO_REUSEADDR ,
.Dv SO_KEEPALIVE ,
.Dv SO_LINGER
and the low-water marks are accepted and change nothing.
.Pp
.Dv FIONREAD
reports the bytes queued on a stream, or the length of the next message;
.Dv TIOCOUTQ
the bytes the peer has not read.
.Xr fstat 2
reports
.Dv S_IFSOCK
and the socket's inode, the number
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /fd
shows as
.Dq socket:[inode] .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr socket 2 ,
.Xr socketcall 2
